<!-- ACT 1: Person in a moment + Impossible framing -->
    <h2 id="idea">The Idea That Everyone Said Was Wrong</h2>

    <p>Mario Molina was a young postdoc in 1973. He worked in a basement lab at the University of California, Irvine, with his advisor Sherwood Rowland. They were studying something most people had never heard of. Chlorofluorocarbons. CFCs. The miracle chemical used in refrigerators, air conditioners, and aerosol cans. Non-toxic. Non-flammable. Stable. A billion-dollar industry.</p>

    <p>One afternoon, Molina drew a diagram on a blackboard. The molecule was stable. That was its selling point. But Molina and Rowland realized something the industry had not considered. If CFCs are stable enough to survive in the lower atmosphere, they drift upward. They reach the stratosphere. And there, ultraviolet light breaks them apart, releasing chlorine atoms that destroy ozone.</p>

    <p>Rowland looked at the diagram. He told Molina: "This means the end of the world."</p>

    <p>They published their findings in <em>Nature</em> in 1974. The reaction was cold. Industry dismissed them. Other scientists questioned their data. Governments took no action. In 2026, a paper like that would spread across the internet in hours. In 1974, it took years for anyone to listen.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: The obstacle + The low point -->
    <h2 id="waiting">The Waiting Years</h2>

    <p>By the early 1980s, Molina and Rowland had been warning for nearly a decade. The US banned CFCs in aerosol cans in 1978. But Europe did not. Other countries did not. CFC production actually increased. DuPont, the largest manufacturer, argued the science was inconclusive. The industry knew the economic stakes. Billions of dollars in patents and production lines. They funded their own studies to create doubt.</p>

    <p>Molina watched his predictions play out from a small office. He gave talks at conferences where half the audience did not believe him. He testified before committees that did not act. He had discovered a global threat and could not make anyone care.</p>

    <p>This is the part of the story that does not make it into the textbooks. The waiting. The being ignored. The knowing you are right while the world does nothing.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: The breakthrough -->
    <h2 id="satellite">The Satellite Data They Almost Ignored</h2>

    <p>In 1985, a British research team led by Joe Farman published data from the Halley Bay station in Antarctica. They had been measuring ozone levels since 1957. They saw something impossible. Every spring, the ozone dropped by 60%. A hole was forming. The satellite data had been flagged as errors. The computer system was programmed to reject readings this extreme as anomalies.</p>

    <p>Farman's paper changed everything. A hole in the ozone layer. The size of North America. Growing every year. The science was no longer theoretical. It was visible. You could measure it. You could map it. The industry's argument that the science was inconclusive collapsed. The world could see the sky falling.</p>

    <p>In 1987, 197 countries gathered in Montreal. They agreed to ban CFCs. The timeline was aggressive. Fast phase-out. Staggered deadlines for developing countries. A fund to help poor nations transition. By 1996, CFC production in developed countries had stopped entirely.</p>

    <p>The treaty had a secret weapon: it could revise itself. As new science emerged, the treaty could strengthen its targets without starting over. In the 1990s, scientists discovered that halons, used in fire extinguishers, were even worse. The treaty absorbed it. It grew stronger without breaking.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: The result -->
    <h2 id="recovery">The Recovery Nobody Reports</h2>

    <p>Today, the ozone hole is healing. Slowly, but measurably. The latest projections show the ozone layer returning to 1980 levels by 2040 over most of the world. The Antarctic hole is expected to close by 2066.</p>

    <p>Two million cases of skin cancer have been prevented by the Montreal Protocol. That number will grow to tens of millions by the end of the century. CFC levels in the atmosphere are declining. The hole is shrinking.</p>

    <blockquote>"The Montreal Protocol is arguably the most successful environmental treaty in history. It has achieved near-universal ratification and effectively phased out 98% of ozone-depleting substances."<br>- United Nations Environment Programme</blockquote>

    <p>There is unfinished business. Illegal CFC production still occurs in some regions. But the core story holds. A global environmental crisis was identified. The cause was traced to specific chemicals. Scientists were ignored for years. Then the evidence became undeniable. And once the world acted, the thing began to heal.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: You-relevance -->
    <h2 id="victory">The Invisible Victory</h2>

    <p>Here is what I want you to think about the next time you walk outside. You step into the sun. You feel the warmth. You do not think about UV radiation. You do not check the UV index with fear. You do not wonder if the sun is safe for your children.</p>

    <p>Your parents might have. By the 1990s, news about the ozone hole was everywhere. People in Australia were told to stay indoors between 10am and 2pm. Schools changed schedules. Sunscreen became a public health campaign.</p>

    <p>You do not think about any of that. Because a young postdoc drew a diagram on a blackboard. He and his advisor spent a decade being ignored. Then the hole appeared. Then the world acted. And the problem that seemed like a death sentence for the planet is now a footnote in your daily life.</p>

    <p>The ozone layer is healing. You live your life without worrying about the sky disappearing. That is not luck. That is a man who drew a diagram on a blackboard, refused to be ignored, and was eventually proven right.</p>
